\begin{quote}
We call two weight assignments \emph{D}1 and \emph{D}2 on \emph{HX,Y
k}-wise equivalent if for all \emph{W ⊆X} of size \emph{k}, the marginal
weight assignments \emph{D}1\emph{,W} and \emph{D}2\emph{,W} (of
\emph{D}1 and \emph{D}2) over \emph{HW,Y} are identical.

\textbf{2.2} \textbf{Quantum Computation}
\end{quote}

A quantum algorithm is an algorithm executed on a quantum computer that
produces a classical output. Most of the background in quantum
computation needed to understand this paper is for the proof of Theorem
3.1. Since this proof appears in Appendix B, we present the necessary
background there. In the meantime, we recall a couple basic facts about
quantum computation, and refer the reader to {[}NC00{]} for a more
thorough discussion.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Fact 1.} Any classical computation can be implemented on a
quantum computer.

\textbf{Fact 2.} Any function that has an efficient classical algorithm
computing it can be implemented efficiently as a quantum-accessible
oracle.
\end{quote}

\textbf{Fact 3.} Given a quantum algorithm \emph{A} with oracle access
to an oracle \emph{O}, each oracle \emph{O} defines a probability
distribution of the outputs of \emph{A}. Hence, any weight assignment of
oracles leads to a weight assignment of outputs of \emph{A}, and if two
weight assignments \emph{D}1 and \emph{D}2 are a distance \emph{ϵ}
apart, the weight assignment of the outputs of \emph{A} under these
distributions are a distance at most \emph{ϵ} apart.

4

\begin{quote}
\textbf{2.3} \textbf{Cryptographic Primitives}

Here we briefly outline a few cryptographic primitives, and refer to
Appendix A for details. All primitives depend on a security parameter
\emph{n}.
\end{quote}

An encryption scheme E is a triple of PPT algorithms
(E\emph{.}Gen\emph{,} E\emph{.}Enc\emph{,} E\emph{.}Dec), where
E\emph{.}Gen(1\emph{n}) generates secret/public keys (sk\emph{,} pk),
E\emph{.}Encpk encrypts a message, and E\emph{.}Decsk decrypts a
ciphertext. We use the indistinguishability under chosen plaintext
attack (IND-CPA) notion of security {[}GM84{]}.

\begin{quote}
An identity-based encryption (IBE) scheme IBE = (IBE\emph{.}Gen\emph{,}
IBE\emph{.}Extract\emph{,} IBE\emph{.}Enc\emph{,} IBE\emph{.}Dec) is a
4-tuple of PPT algorithms where IBE\emph{.}Gen(1\emph{n}) generates
master secret/public keys (msk\emph{,} mpk), IBE\emph{.}Extractmsk
generates secret keys for given identities, IBE\emph{.}Encmpk encrypts a
message to an identity, and IBE\emph{.}Dec decrypts a ciphertext sent to
an identity by using the corresponding secret key. We use the
indistinguishability under chosen plaintext attack (IND-ID-CPA) notion
of security {[}BF01{]}.

A signature scheme S = (S\emph{.}Gen\emph{,} S\emph{.}Sign\emph{,}
S\emph{.}Ver) is a triple of PPT algorithms where
S\emph{.}Gen(1\emph{n}) generates secret/public keys (sk\emph{,} pk),
S\emph{.}Signsk signs a message, and S\emph{.}Verpk verifies a
signature. We use the existential unforgeablility under chosen message
attack (UF-CMA) notion of security {[}GMR88{]}.

A pre-image sampleable function (PSF) is a quadruple of algorithms F =
(F\emph{.}Gen\emph{,} F\emph{.}Sample\emph{, f, f−}1) where F\emph{.}Gen
generates private/public keys (sk\emph{,} pk), \emph{f}pk is a function,
F\emph{.}Sample samples \emph{x} from a sk(\emph{y}) samples from
\emph{D} conditioned on \emph{f}pk(\emph{x}) = \emph{y}. distribution
\emph{D} such that \emph{f}pk(\emph{x}) is uniform, and \emph{f−}1\\
For security, we use the notion of one-wayness.

A trapdoor permutation (TDP) is a special case of a PSF where \emph{f}
is bijective and F\emph{.}Sample simply returns a random element in the
domain of \emph{f}pk. Since F\emph{.}Sample is already defined, we omit
it from the specification.

\textbf{2.4} \textbf{Random Oracle Model}

In the Random Oracle Model, we assume the existence of a random function
\emph{H}, and give all parties oracle access to this function. If
\emph{A} makes queries to an oracle \emph{H}, we denote this as
\emph{AH}. If a system \emph{S} uses a random oracle in its
specification, we denote this as \emph{SH}. The algorithms comprising
any cryptographic protocol can use \emph{H}, as can the adversary. Thus
we modify the security games for all cryptographic systems to allow the
adversary to make random oracle queries.

When a random oracle scheme is implemented, some suitable hash function
\emph{H} is included in the specification. Any algorithm (adversary
included) now replaces oracle queries with evaluations of this hash
function. In the quantum setting, because a quantum algorithm can
evaluate \emph{H} on an arbitrary superposition, we must allow the
quantum adversary to make quantum queries to the random oracle. We call
this the quantum random oracle model.

\textbf{3} \textbf{Distinguishing Oracles With Quantum Queries}

In this section, we give some tools for arguing that quantum algorithms
cannot distinguish between two distributions of oracles. The trivial way
to bound the ability of any quantum algorithm to distinguish two oracle
distributions \emph{D}1 and \emph{D}2 is to bound the distance between
\emph{D}1 and \emph{D}2 themselves. However, in many cases, the
distributions we are interested in are in fact very far apart, so we
need some new techniques for arguing indistinguishability. First, we
show that \emph{k}-wise equivalent distributions are indistinguishable,
using techniques similar to that of Meyer and Pommersheim {[}MP11{]}:
\end{quote}

