\begin{quote}
We conclude this section with another general approach toward arguing
that no quantum
\end{quote}

algorithm can distinguish between two oracle distributions \emph{D}1 and
\emph{D}2: construct a weight assignment \emph{D}1\emph{,}2 which is
2\emph{q}-wise equivalent to \emph{D}1 (so that the behaviors of
\emph{A} under \emph{D}1 and \emph{D}1\emph{,}2 are the same) and that
is a distance at most \emph{ϵ} from \emph{D}2 (so that the behaviors of
\emph{A} under \emph{D}1\emph{,}2 and \emph{D}2 are \emph{ϵ}-close).
Then the behaviors of \emph{A} under \emph{D}1 and \emph{D}2 are also
\emph{ϵ}-close. We formalize this latter idea with a new pseudometric on
oracle distributions:

