\begin{minipage}[t]{\linewidth}\raggedright
In this section, we define a class of distributions on oracles in
\emph{HX,Y} called semi-constant distributions. Our motivation for these
distributions is to support quantum Full Domain Hash-type arguments,

where a random value is inserted into a small but significant fraction
of oracle inputs. The following

\begin{quote}
definition captures the essence of the this idea:
\end{quote}

\textbf{Definition 4.1.} \emph{Define} SC\emph{λ, the semi-constant
distribution, as the distribution over HX,Y resulting from the following
process:}
\end{minipage}} \\
\bottomrule()
\end{longtable}

\begin{quote}
\emph{• First, pick a random element y from Y.}

\emph{• For each x ∈X, do one of the following:}\\
\textbf{--} \emph{With probability λ, set H}(\emph{x}) = \emph{y. We
call x a distinguished input to H.}

\textbf{--} \emph{Otherwise, set H}(\emph{x}) \emph{to be a random
element in Y.}

First, notice that SC0 is the uniform distribution, since in this case,
the probability that an input is distinguished is 0. We bound the
ability of a quantum algorithm to distinguish between SC\emph{λ} and SC0
= \emph{U} using the ideas of Section 3.
\end{quote}

\textbf{Lemma 4.2.} \emph{Fix k. For each k pairs} (\emph{xi,
ri})\emph{, the probability} Pr\emph{H←}SC\emph{λ}{[}\emph{H}(\emph{xi})
= \emph{ri∀i ∈\{}1\emph{, ..., k\}}{]} \emph{is a degree-k polynomial in
λ whose first derivative is 0 at λ} = 0\emph{.}

8

\begin{quote}
This, proved in Appendix C, shows that for any \emph{qH}, we can set
\emph{d} = \emph{k} = 2\emph{qH} and ∆= 2 in the assumptions of Theorem
3.2. We immediately get:

\textbf{Corollary 4.3.} \emph{The distribution of outputs of a quantum
algorithm making qH queries to an oracle drawn from} SC\emph{λ is at
most a distance}\uline{8 3}\emph{q}4 \emph{Hλ}2 \emph{away from the case
when the oracle is drawn from the uniform distribution.}

We note that using standard quantum query results, it is possible to
prove this corollary for a bound that is first-order in \emph{λ}.
However, we will see in Section 5 that we need a bound that is
second-order in \emph{λ} for our security results.

We do not know if this bound is tight. In Appendix C, we adapt the
collision search algorithm of Brassard et al. {[}BHT97{]} to SC\emph{λ},
obtaining a distinguishing probability of Ω(\emph{q}3 \emph{Hλ}2).

\textbf{5} \textbf{Quantum Security Arguments}

In this section, we explore the random oracle proof technique of Bellare
and Rogaway {[}BR93{]}. In this technique, we plug a challenge \emph{c}
into a randomly chosen hash query, and hope that the adversary uses
\emph{c} to break the system. If so, we can use the adversary to solve
the underlying problem.

In the quantum setting, the straight-forward application of this
technique breaks down. The adversary can now query the hash function on
a superposition of exponentially many inputs. If we plug \emph{c} to one
of those inputs, the probability that the adversary uses \emph{c} is
exponentially small.

We instead plug \emph{c} into some small but significant fraction of the
inputs, so that the oracle is distributed according to SC\emph{λ}. As we
have shown, a quantum algorithm cannot detect that this oracle is not
random, but now the adversary uses \emph{c} with significant
probability.

\textbf{5.1} \textbf{Identity-Based Encryption from Lattices}

Here we prove the security of the IBE scheme from Gentry et al.
{[}GPV08{]}. Their scheme is constructed from an encryption scheme E =
(E\emph{.}Gen\emph{,} E\emph{.}Enc\emph{,} E\emph{.}Dec), for which
there exists a trapdoor allowing the computation of secret keys from
public keys.

More specifically, let F = (F\emph{.}Gen\emph{,} F\emph{.}Sample\emph{,
f, f−}1) be a pre-image sampleable function (PSF). Suppose
E\emph{.}Gen(1\emph{n}) works as follows: generate (msk\emph{,} mpk)
\emph{←}F\emph{.}Gen(1\emph{n}). F\emph{.}Sample(1\emph{n}), and compute
pk = \emph{f}mpk(sk). Output (sk\emph{,} (pk\emph{,} mpk)). Then, sample
sk \emph{←}
\end{quote}

Gentry et al. give such an encryption scheme based on the hardness of
lattice problems. Their se-curity reduction treats the adversary as a
black box, so their proof holds in the quantum setting. They then prove
the security of the IBE scheme IBE = (IBE\emph{.}Gen =
F\emph{.}Gen\emph{,} IBE\emph{.}Extract\emph{H,} IBE\emph{.}Enc\emph{H,}
IBE\emph{.}Dec) where \emph{H} maps identities to public keys of E and:

\begin{quote}
\emph{•} IBE\emph{.}Extractmsk(id)\emph{H}= \emph{f−}1
msk(\emph{H}(id))\\
\emph{•} IBE\emph{.}Encmpk(id\emph{, m})\emph{H}=
E\emph{.}Enc\emph{H}(id)(\emph{m})\\
\emph{•} IBE\emph{.}Decskid(\emph{c}) = E\emph{.}Decskid(\emph{c})
\end{quote}

\textbf{Theorem 5.1.} \emph{Let} E \emph{and} F \emph{be as above, and
suppose that} E \emph{is quantum IND-CPA-secure. If we model H as a
random oracle, then} IBE \emph{is quantum IND-ID-CPA-secure.}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Proof}\emph{.} Let \emph{A} be a quantum adversary making
\emph{qH} hash queries, \emph{qE} extract queries queries, that breaks
IBE with advantage \emph{ϵ}.
\end{quote}

