id\emph{∗•} For each level \emph{i}, generate some random quantities
\emph{Ri}.

\emph{•} For each level \emph{i}, simulate a separate random oracle for
identities at that level. For \emph{i}, guess which query number
\emph{qi} will contain the hash of the level-\emph{i} parent of
id\emph{∗}.

\emph{•} Answer the \emph{j}th random oracle query at level \emph{i} on
id\emph{i,j} as follows: if \emph{j} = \emph{qi}, output \emph{Ri}.
Otherwise, output a random value.

\emph{•} Answer secret key queries on id in some special way, but fail
if for all prefixes id\emph{i} of id, id\emph{i} = id\emph{i,qi}. That
is, fail if \emph{H}(id\emph{i}) = \emph{Ri}.

\emph{•} When the adversary generates the identity id\emph{∗}, we
succeed if both the adversary succeeds and if id\emph{∗}is at level
\emph{w} and all prefixes id\emph{∗i}of id\emph{∗}satisfy id\emph{∗i}=
id\emph{i,qi}.

We now show how to prove security by repeatedly applying the arguments
of Theorem 5.1.
