\textbf{6} \textbf{Simulating Random Oracles}

In this last section, we explain how to efficiently simulate random
oracles. All quantum random oracle proofs to date require the reduction
algorithm to have quantum access to a random oracle, but a truly random
oracle requires exponentially many bits of randomness to construct. We
show that this is not a problem:

\textbf{Theorem 6.1.} \emph{Any quantum algorithm A making quantum
queries to random oracles Oi can be efficiently simulated by a quantum
algorithm B, which has the same output distribution, but makes no
queries.}
\end{quote}

\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 0\tabcolsep) * \real{1.0000}}@{}}
\toprule()
\begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\begin{quote}
\textbf{Proof}\emph{.} We construct an algorithm \emph{B} which
simulates \emph{A}, and answers queries to oracle \emph{Oi} with
evaluations of efficient functions \emph{fi}. Boneh et al. {[}BDF+11{]}
use pseudorandom functions (PRF) for the \emph{fi}. At first glance,
this seems like the only option, as we need a function \emph{fi} that
cannot be distinguished from random.
\end{quote}

Notice, however, that \emph{fi} need not be secure against all
adversaries, just the adversary we are simulating. We know that our
adversary makes \emph{qi} queries to oracle \emph{Oi}, so it suffices to
have \emph{fi} be PRFs secure for up to \emph{qi} quantum queries. In
the classical setting, \emph{qi}-wise independent functions (functions
that are \emph{qi}-wise equivalent to a random function) serve as
\emph{perfectly} secure PRFs for up to \emph{qi} classical queries. We
could hope that something similar holds in the quantum world: indeed,
according to Theorem 3.1, if \emph{fi} is 2\emph{qi}-wise equivalent to
a random function, then the behavior of our adversary is the same when
the oracle is random and when it is \emph{fi}. Thus if the \emph{fi} are
2\emph{qi}-wise independent, algorithm \emph{A}, as a subroutine of
\emph{B}, behaves identically to the case where \emph{A} is given truly
random oracles. Hence, the output distribution of \emph{B} is identical
to that of \emph{A}.

\begin{quote}
Efficient constructions of \emph{k}-wise independent functions have been
known for some time {[}Jof74, KM94{]}, and they have been used
extensively in the derandomization literature {[}Lub85, ABI86, KM93{]}.
One common approach to construct a \emph{k}-wise independent function
\emph{f} from \emph{X} to \emph{Y} is to assume that \emph{N} =
\emph{\textbar Y\textbar{}} is a prime power and interpret \emph{Y} as
the field F\emph{N}. Then define a matrix \emph{C} with the following
properties:

\emph{•} The entries are elements in F\emph{N}.

\emph{•} There are \emph{\textbar X\textbar{}} rows and some small
number \emph{r} of columns.

\emph{•} Each subset of \emph{k} rows is linearly independent.

One such example is the Vandermonde matrix, which is used by Alon et
al.{[}ABI86{]}. To define the function \emph{f}, we then pick a random
vector \emph{v} in F\emph{r N}. \emph{f}(\emph{x}) is then the
\emph{x}th element of of the vector \emph{Cv}. Since any \emph{k} rows
of \emph{C} are linearly independent, any \emph{k} elements of \emph{Cv}
are independent, and hence \emph{f} is \emph{k}-wise independent. The
key to making this efficient is that to compute \emph{f}(\emph{x}), we
only need the \emph{x}th row of \emph{C}, which we can compute on the
fly.
\end{quote}

