\begin{quote}
Here we explore the problem of finding a collision in an oracle drawn
from a semi-constant distribution SC\emph{λ} over \emph{HX,Y}. Our
motivation for studying the collision problem is as follows: a classical
algorithm making \emph{qH} queries can only distinguish SC\emph{λ} from
random with probability \emph{O}(\emph{λ}2\emph{q}2 \emph{H}), since it
can only distinguish the two cases if it happens to query two
distinguished points. Further, querying random points, and outputting 1
if a collision is found achieves this bound. Thus, a collision search is
the best way to distinguish SC\emph{λ} from random in the classical
setting, and the same may also be true in the quantum setting
\end{quote}

requires (with high probability) finding a collision in the
distinguished inputs. Let \emph{N} = \emph{\textbar Y\textbar{}} be the
number of elements in \emph{Y}, and assume that \emph{λ
\textgreater\textgreater{}}1 \emph{N}so that finding a collision

Let \emph{c} be the minimum constant such that SC\emph{λ} is
indistinguishable from uniform with except with probability
\emph{O}(\emph{qcλ}2). Corollary 4.3 shows us that \emph{c ≤}4. We will
now show that \emph{c ≥}3, using
\end{minipage}} \\
\bottomrule()
