\begin{quote}
\textbf{E} \textbf{Proof of Theorem 5.3}
\end{quote}

We prove Theorem 5.3, which states that the FDH signature scheme is
secure in the quantum random oracle model if the underlying trapdoor
permutation is secure against quantum adversaries.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Proof of Theorem 5.3.} Suppose towards contradiction that there
is a quantum adversary \emph{A} making \emph{qH} hash queries, \emph{qS}
signature queries, that breaks S with probability \emph{ϵ}. Assume
without loss of generality that \emph{A} never tries to forge a
signature on a message it already received a secret key for.

Let Game0 be the standard attack game for S: the challenger generates
(sk\emph{,} pk) from S\emph{.}Gen, and sends pk to the adversary. The
adversary can make (classical) signature queries on messages \emph{mi},
to which the challenger responds with S\emph{.}Sign\emph{H}
sk(\emph{mi}), and (quantum) hash queries to the random oracle \emph{H}.

pk(\emph{m∗, σ∗}) = accept. \emph{A} wins if it can produce a pair
(\emph{m∗, σ∗}) such that \emph{m∗̸}= \emph{mi} for any \emph{i}, and
S\emph{.}Ver\emph{H}\\
The success probability in Game0 is \emph{ϵ}.

Assume without loss of generality that \emph{A} never asks for a
signature on \emph{m∗}. Let \emph{A′}be the following algorithm that
makes quantum queries to an oracle \emph{H}, and simulates the
interaction between \emph{A} and the challenger: generate (sk\emph{,}
pk) from S\emph{.}Gen, send pk to \emph{A}. Run \emph{A}, forwarding all
hash queries to \emph{H}, and answering extraction queries using
S\emph{.}Sign\emph{H}. The output of \emph{A′}is (S\emph{.}Ver\emph{H}
pk(\emph{m∗, σ∗})\emph{, m∗, σ∗, Q}) where \emph{Q} is the list of
signature queries made by \emph{A}. We can now think of Game0 as
follows: run \emph{A′}with a random oracle \emph{H} to obtain (\emph{m∗,
σ∗, Q}). Report that the game is won if and only if
S\emph{.}Ver\emph{H}\\
pk(\emph{m∗, σ∗}) = accept. The number of queries to \emph{H} made by
\emph{A′} is \emph{qH}for direct queries, \emph{qS} for queries through
the signature algorithm, and 1 for the check S\emph{.}Ver\emph{H}
pk(\emph{m∗, σ∗}), for a total of \emph{qH} + \emph{qS} + 1 queries.

Let \emph{λ ∈}(0\emph{,} 1) to be chosen later. Let \emph{M} be the
message space. Let Game1 be Game0, except that we choose a subset
\emph{X} of \emph{M} as follows: for each \emph{m ∈M}, we put \emph{m}
in \emph{X} with probability\emph{λ}. If \emph{A} asks for a signature
on a message in \emph{X}, abort and \emph{A} loses. If the message
\emph{A} attempts to
\end{quote}

