Now we are ready to define an algorithm \emph{B} what inverts \emph{f}.
Give \emph{B} access to two random oracles \emph{O}1 and \emph{O}2.
\emph{O}1 maps messages to inputs to \emph{f}. \emph{O}2 maps messages
to bits, where the probability of outputting 1 is \emph{λ}. On input
(pk\emph{, y}), \emph{B} works as follows:

\emph{•} Send pk to \emph{A}, and simulate \emph{A}, playing the role of
challenger to \emph{A}.
\end{quote}

26

\begin{quote}
\emph{•} Construct the (quantum) oracle \emph{H} such that

\emph{H}(\emph{m}) = \emph{y} if \emph{O}2(\emph{m}) = 1\\
\emph{f}pk(\emph{O}1(\emph{m})) otherwise

\emph{•} When \emph{A} makes a signature query on a message \emph{mi},
compute \emph{O}2(\emph{mi}). If the result is 1, abort. Otherwise,
return \emph{σi} = \emph{O}1(\emph{mi}).\\
\emph{•} When \emph{A} returns a forgery candidate (\emph{m, σ}), test
if \emph{O}2(\emph{m}) = 1 and \emph{f}pk(\emph{σ}) = \emph{y}. If so,
output \emph{σ}. Otherwise, abort.
\end{quote}

Using an analysis similar to that of Theorem 5.1, we get that the
advantage of \emph{B} is at least

\begin{quote}
\emph{ϵ}2\\
\emph{ℓ}(\emph{qH, qS})
\end{quote}

